From 8864fd0aecf21fe9e3cfcd83a8ef33cb7e885fd4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: REPLACE-ME <you@example>
Date: Tue, 06 Oct 2026 14:34:02 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] avfilter: add dovi_apply, applying DV RPU reshaping curves

Dolby Vision base layers are not the picture their metadata describes: the RPU's
reshaping curves sit between the coded components and the signal the content was
authored for. Nothing in tree evaluates those curves on the CPU today -
libavcodec only parses them into AVDOVIDataMapping (dovi_rpudec.c), vf_showinfo
prints them, and vf_libplacebo applies them inside the GPU renderer via
pl_map_avframe_ex(.map_dovi=...). A CPU pipeline therefore cannot get HDR10+
(SMPTE ST 2094-40) metadata out of a Dolby Vision source at all: measuring the base
layer directly describes a picture nobody graded.

This filter closes that step. Per pixel it takes the stream's own component planes,
rebuilds chroma onto the luma grid by a DECLARED method, evaluates the per-component
piecewise function (polynomial or MMR, coefficients divided by 2**coef_log2_denom,
pivots compared in the source's coded domain), then applies the DM's own
ycc_to_rgb_matrix - which dovi_meta.h documents as acting "before PQ linearization" -
and emits AV_PIX_FMT_GBRP at the input depth in the PQ code domain, tagged
BT.2020 / SMPTE ST 2084 / full range. That output domain is what pairs with the
hdr10plus producer filter of the earlier patch in this series: the producer applies the
ST 2084 EOTF itself, as ST 2094-40 4.4 requires.

Input is restricted to 4:2:0, and that restriction is enforced rather than implied: the
rebuild indexes chroma as (y >> 1, x >> 1) with cw = (w + 1) / 2, so a 4:2:2 or 4:4:4 frame
would be measured against the wrong samples and the metadata would still serialise, decode and
look fine. 4:2:2 and 4:4:4 deliberately STAY in query_formats(): with only 4:2:0 listed, the
graph would quietly insert a chroma conversion to reach it, which is the same unmarked assumption
the chroma option refuses to make. Listing them means such a frame arrives and is refused here,
with the conversion named as the caller's decision.

The rebuild walks the chroma grid, one cell per 2x2 luma block, so each cell loads its four
bilinear taps once instead of four times. The per-pixel statements were moved verbatim (only
re-indented), which is what makes it arithmetic-identical rather than approximately so:
(in)(v * 0.5 + 0.25) == v >> 1 for every v, and v >> 1 is never outside the chroma plane for a v
inside the picture, so no clamp can move y0/x0 and the weights are exactly 0.25/0.75 by parity.
Verified by comparing whole frames byte for byte against the previous implementation on nine
geometries (1920x1080, the same frame with odd width, odd height and both, and 1x1, 2x2, 3x3,
5x7, 7x5) plus four frames of two real clips. Measured on this box at -O2, interleaved A/B/A,
seven runs per arm and distributions that do not overlap: 127.8 ms -> 109.1 ms per 1920x1080
frame (1.17x by medians, 1.14x by per-arm minima).

Two shapes are refused rather than approximated, all with the reason in the message:

  - ICtCp (signal_color_space == 2), where the transfer to assume for the curve output is
    not carried by the bitstream, and the two candidates do not just differ slightly -
    on the same frame they move average_maxrgb by more than a factor of two. Emitting a
    guess would be indistinguishable downstream from a correct result;
  - a placeholder curve: one component's piecewise function maps its whole declared pivot
    range to a single value. Applying it is not an error and nothing crashes - every pixel
    comes out the same, so the producer downstream measures a flat picture and emits a block
    that serialises, decodes and prints a complete set of ST 2094-40 values. It is refused
    because that output is indistinguishable from a correct one after the fact. The test is
    the one the validated Python kernel this filter mirrors uses: 257 points spread over the
    component's own pivots, refuse when max - min < 1e-3 (p5_applier.py:129-138). It is not
    a hypothetical guard - the same measurement on real clips found such blocks on 30 of 60
    frames of one and 31 of 60 of another, where every usable curve spanned at least 0.54.

A RESIDUAL (dual) LAYER is no longer one of them. It is composed, with el_rebuild / el_neutral
declared by the caller - and refusing to choose those is the same style the two refusals above
use. That is the whole reason the compose could land here: CCM 001 clause 5.4.1 states outright
that resampling "is not a normative part of the present document", so the bitstream does not say
which filter brought the enhancement layer onto the base layer's raster, and clause 5.4.3.2's
"rr = e - nlq_offset" does not settle where the layer's zero residual sits on real material
(the CES 00004 pair's own EL luma median is code 64 while its RPU declares nlq_offset=512 for
all three components - 2.4x apart). Both are therefore AVOptions with no default, refused by
name when undeclared, exactly as p5_transfer is above. The compose itself is measured, not
asserted: against the shipped Python reference on a real paired subject the two pipelines agree
BIT-IDENTICALLY on 98.4355% of 24,883,200 samples (median |delta| = 0), the disagreement is
confined to a thin spatially-local tail, and the teeth arm shows the residual genuinely reaches
the pixels (the composed frame matches the composed reference at mean|delta| 1.653 against 3.598
for the base layer alone). The residual between the two pipelines is EL/BL resampling, which is
the non-normative step above - so the <= 1 code arm is reported as failing rather than loosened.

The probe reads the RPU alone, not the frame, so it costs 3 * 257 evaluations of a polynomial
or a small MMR per frame, allocates nothing, and lives in a function of its own: the per-pixel
reshape path is untouched byte for byte (re-checked against the previous revision of this patch),
which is what keeps the numeric contract asserted elsewhere exactly where it was. Its arithmetic
was executed, not reasoned about: a single-TU harness calling the same dovi_apply_probe_curves()
filter_frame() calls, fed dv84's real RPU (bl_bit_depth 10, coef_log2_denom 23, 9 luma pivots,
8 polynomial pieces), reproduces the validated Python kernel's span/min/max/first for that curve
to 9 decimals (0.658104121 / 0.064753009 / 0.722857129) and returns the expected verdict in four
arms - real curve accepted, luma zeroed refused naming component 0, and a flat MMR refused naming
components 1 and 2 respectively. Not verified: the refusal has not been driven through a real
filtergraph (that needs a tree rebuilt with this source), and the box's single-layer subjects carry
no placeholder block to reach it with - measured, not assumed: walking 60 DM blocks of each,
glowtime refuses 30 and shine 31 (all of them component 0 mapping its domain to 0.0, span 0.0),
pursuit 0, and dv84 0 with its smallest component span 0.658, which is why a rebuilt tree should
not start refusing the existing in-graph material. Compile arms: three, all exit 0, no diagnostic
attributed to the new lines.

The chroma rebuild has no default, because the geometry is part of the result: measured,
nearest and bilinear put J=50 4.49% apart, and a 4:2:0 path moves the median 19.7% against a
4:4:4 reference. Choosing silently would put an unmarked assumption into the metadata, and
the distribution sanity checks do not see it.

Input and output formats differ, so query_formats pins each side separately rather than
using the common-list helper; DOVI side data is dropped from the output by type, since the
generic colour-dependent-property sweep would also strip the HDR10+ side data the next
filter attaches.

Two depth rules follow from that, and both are refusals rather than conversions because the
declared domain is not this filter's to guess:
 - the frame is only accepted at the depth the RPU declares in bl_bit_depth. The curves act on
   the base layer's own code domain, so feeding 12-bit samples through a 1/1023 divisor scales
   every pixel by four and pins the curve output at its ceiling. Measured on a Profile 8 clip
   declared at 10 bit and pushed through format=yuv420p12le: the frames that came out had R =
   4095 and G = 0 across two thirds of the picture, five distinct codes in the whole frame, and
   the producer downstream reported maxscl = average_maxrgb = 100000/100000. That serialises and
   decodes cleanly, so nothing after this filter could have told.
 - the codes WRITTEN are scaled by the depth of the output buffer, not of the input frame. The
   format list offers gbrp10le and gbrp12le on both sides, so a graph may legitimately negotiate
   different depths; using the input depth for the output range left dv84's reshaped picture in
   codes 0..754 inside a 12-bit buffer - four times too small - which the producer then read as
   maxscl 18/100000 for a frame it measures as 8499/100000 at the matching depth. With both rules
   in place, the same reshaped picture measured at 10 bit and at 12 bit agrees to within one
   10-bit code step (|d maxscl| 22 and 24 units against a ~57-unit step at that brightness,
   |d average| 0).

Verification (see README): the file compiles warning-free as one translation unit
(cl -W3 -std:c17, MSVC 19.51) against this revision, under the same flags as two control arms -
upstream's own vf_setparams.c unmodified, and the vf_hdr10plus.c of the previous patch. All three
emit only the same pre-existing intmath.h C4146 pair, so no diagnostic is attributable to these
lines. The arithmetic was checked numerically: frames of two real Profile 8 clips were pushed
through this filter and then through the hdr10plus producer, and the resulting statistics match an
independent reference implementation of the same chain with maxscl and all nine percentiles
identical, average_maxrgb differing only on a .5 rounding tie (llround away from zero versus
half-to-even). It has also been built and run as part of a whole tree (this revision, MSVC 19.51 +
GNU Make, --toolchain=msvc --disable-x86asm): both filters appear in -filters, two frames of a real
Profile 8 clip pass dovi_apply -> hdr10plus inside an actual filtergraph, and the frames carry
per-frame ST 2094-40 metadata read back with structured values (application version 1,
num_distribution_maxrgb_percentiles 9). The refusals are graph-tested too, one frame each:
4:2:2 and 4:4:4 input, a frame without DOVI side data, and a frame deeper than
the RPU declares all reach filter_frame and come back as errors rather than crashing, and omitting
`chroma` fails at init. A DUAL-LAYER clip is no longer in that list: it is composed when the caller
declares `el_rebuild` and `el_neutral`, and refused by name when either is missing (EINVAL) or when
no enhancement layer is supplied (ENOTSUP) - three distinguishable outcomes, tested. Two whole-tree
builds were judged, with x86 asm disabled and with it
enabled through nasm 2.16.01: the same 22 graph assertions pass on both, and every metadata value
they print is identical between them. Not verified: SIMD (the loop is scalar double; ~18 Mpix/s at
-O2 in a single translation unit on this box - no timing is claimed from either tree build), and a
genuinely 12-bit source: no 12-bit Dolby Vision subject was available here, so the 12-bit arms are
measured on depth conversions of 10-bit clips and on samples written by the suite.

Signed-off-by: REPLACE-ME <replace-me@example.com>

